<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stedingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stedingen is a marshland district on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, settled by free peasant farmers in the 12th century. When the Stedinger refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> and dues to the archbishop of Bremen, they were declared heretics and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> was preached against them, ending with their defeat at the Battle of Altenesch in 1234. Their land was then divided between Bremen and the counts of Oldenburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stedingen.jpg" alt="Stedingen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, possibly Johann Conrad Musculus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Unterweser.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stedingen lies north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> in the delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. In 1106 five Dutchmen agreed with Archbishop Frederick I of Bremen to settle the swampy land south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunte">Hunte</a> on both banks of the river. The settlers held their farms in free hereditary possession and owed the archbishop a yearly pfennig, the eleventh sheaf of their harvests and a tenth of their livestock, but were otherwise free of any secular lord. Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> peasants came in large numbers, dug ditches to drain the moorland and built dikes against floods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Archbishop Gerard I (1210–1219), his kinsman Otto I, Count of Oldenburg, built the fortresses of Lechtenburg and Lineburg in Stedingen to enforce church and feudal discipline. The Stedingers, holding to their original agreement, refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> and forced labour, accused the count's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of rape and kidnapping, and resolved at their Thing to proclaim total independence, fortify the roads and form militias. His successor Gerard II built Schlutterburg Castle on the border for his brother, Lord Herman II of Lippe, who was killed when the peasants defeated the archbishop's knights at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1229.</p><p>On 17 March 1230 a council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> found the Stedingers guilty of disobedience, sacrilege and murder, and of worshipping wax images and consorting with evil spirits; an interdict followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, preached by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a> with the same spiritual rewards as for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. In 1233 the crusaders massacred most of the people of the poorly defended east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, burning the few survivors at the stake, but were driven back from the west bank on 6 July 1233.</p><p>In 1234 a new army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-duke-of-brabant">Henry I, Duke of Brabant</a>, met the Stedingers led by Bolko von Bardenfleth, Tammo von Huntrop and Detmar tom Diek. One force was broken at a fortified gate when a single knight rode through to its rear, and the rest were defeated after heavy resistance at Altenesch. The Saxon Chronicle of Reppichau saw their end as God's judgement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stedingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts expanded over the marshes towards the Weser and the Jade, fighting the free Frisian peasants of <b>Stedingen</b>, who were crushed in a crusade in 1234.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">County of Oldenburg</a></li></ul></section>
